12 Can you describe a specific challenge you faced during this implementation and how you resolved it?

   One significant challenge I faced was during a production deployment where the new application pods were not becoming 
healthy after deployment. Jenkins completed the pipeline successfully, but Kubernetes was unable to route traffic to the 
new pods because the readiness probe kept failing.

I started by checking the pod status using kubectl get pods and then examined the logs with kubectl logs. I found that 
the application required more time to establish the database connection during startup, while the readiness probe was 
checking the health endpoint too early.

To resolve the issue, I increased the readiness probe's initialDelaySeconds and timeoutSeconds to match the application's 
startup time. I tested the changes in the staging environment, verified that the pods became healthy, and then deployed 
the updated configuration to production using Jenkins and Helm.

After the fix, the deployment completed successfully with zero downtime, and we did not encounter the same issue in 
subsequent releases. We also documented the startup requirements and standardized health probe configurations across
all microservices.

14 could you share your experience with monitoring and observability tools like Prometheus and Grafana? How did you 
utilize these tools to improve system performance and incident response?


In my previous project, I used Prometheus and Grafana to monitor our Java Spring Boot microservices running on Amazon EKS. 
Our goal was to gain real-time visibility into application performance, Kubernetes cluster health, and infrastructure 
utilization so that we could detect and resolve issues before they impacted users.

We deployed Prometheus in the EKS cluster to collect metrics from Kubernetes components, worker nodes, and application pods. 
For the Spring Boot application, we enabled Spring Boot Actuator metrics and configured Prometheus to scrape those metrics 
automatically.

Grafana was used to create dashboards for key metrics such as:

CPU and memory utilization of nodes and pods
Pod restart count and CrashLoopBackOff events
Application response time and request rate
HTTP error rates (4xx and 5xx)
EKS node health and cluster resource utilization

We also configured Prometheus Alertmanager to send alerts to our team's Slack channel and email whenever critical 
thresholds were exceeded, such as high CPU usage, pod failures, or node resource exhaustion.

One production incident involved a sudden increase in application response time during peak business hours. 
Grafana showed that CPU utilization on the application pods had reached nearly 90%, and Prometheus confirmed a spike
in request traffic.

To resolve the issue, I analyzed the metrics, verified there were no application errors, and enabled the Horizontal 
Pod Autoscaler (HPA) with appropriate CPU utilization thresholds. As traffic increased, Kubernetes automatically scaled 
the number of pods, reducing CPU utilization and bringing application response times back to normal.

We also reviewed resource requests and limits to ensure better resource allocation and prevent unnecessary throttling.

As a result, the application remained stable during peak traffic, incidents were detected much faster through automated 
alerts, and the operations team could respond proactively instead of waiting for users to report issues


